### Iteration 1 — Encapsulation
```js
// 🏁 Sprint 1  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Sprint Goal: Demonstrate encapsulation by hiding data & behavior
// inside a reusable ES6 class (a.k.a. module).
// Product Backlog Item (PBI-001): “As a developer I want a UserCard
// component whose internal DOM and state are private so that consumers
// cannot break invariants.”
// Definition of Done: class exports only public API, passes unit test.
//
class UserCard {
  #name;            // private field (encapsulated)
  #avatarUrl;       // private field

  constructor(name, avatarUrl) {
    this.#name = name;
    this.#avatarUrl = avatarUrl;
  }

  render(container) {
    const card = document.createElement('div');
    card.className = 'user-card';
    card.innerHTML = `
      <img src="${this.#avatarUrl}" alt="Avatar of ${this.#name}">
      <h2>${this.#name}</h2>`;
    container.appendChild(card);
  }
}

// Sprint Review note: Stakeholders loved that internal fields (#name, #avatarUrl)
// are inaccessible from the outside => low coupling, high cohesion.
```
*Retrospective highlight:* Including the new ES private-field syntax boosted code readability; our **velocity** was 5 story points.

